Policy response for the quick exploratory lab-meeting PNG scenario. It is based only on the frozen snapshot at /tmp/rrs-exemplars/candidate/skills/research-repo-standard/. I read SKILL.md, references/figures.md, prerequisites.md, governance.md, analysis.md, configuration.md and data.md, and the README checklist section of bootstrap.md. I did not view the exemplar images, because they only apply to publication delivery. No files were created or edited.

**1. Skill discovery (the missing AGENTS.md does not matter)**
- The standard applies because the repository already follows it (SKILL.md Scope). Discovery does not depend on an AGENTS.md.
- I resolve the skill through the host's own skill listing or resolver by its exact name, `research-repo-standard:research-repo-standard`.
- I check its reported provenance (marketplace source and installed commit) against the plugin entry in the project README. If the README has no entry, I match the source the user approved; if there is none, I ask.
- A file on disk, or a repository that names the skill, is not evidence that it resolved. At first use I record the host, the resolver, the resolved source and the invocation evidence.
- If it does not resolve, I report the exact name, host, resolver tried and result. I then ask for authorization before installing (`/plugin marketplace add lucascamillomd/research-repo-standard`, then `/plugin install research-repo-standard@research-repo-standard`). I never imitate the skill from memory.
- I never create AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md or CODEX.md, and never copy SKILL.md or the references into the repository. I start no adoption assessment or migration. Any gaps go into the report as follow-up.
- I do not resolve `nature-figure`, `scientific-critical-thinking` or the superpowers skills up front. Each is resolved only right before work that needs it.

**2. Context and classification**
- I check git status and read the README, docs/LAB_NOTEBOOK.md, any existing figure record (docs/FIGURE_CONTRACT.md) and docs/ANALYSIS_PLAN.md (for analysis status).
- I also read config/datasets.yaml and the data dictionary for the processed dataset, config/analysis/figures.yaml and run.yaml, the existing src/<pkg>/figures/ code, the figure Snakemake rules and Make targets, and nearby tests.
- A figure is result-affecting work inside the approved design, so it falls under the **standard gate**. The user's request is the authorization. There is no brainstorming, spec, plan or second confirmation.
- Scope is **exploratory delivery**. That means:
  - no pre-plot contract with all its fields;
  - no `nature-figure`;
  - no bundled exemplars;
  - no SVG, PDF or TIFF exports, and no assembly.
- Existing approved delivery requirements still apply. If this plot is already a contracted publication figure, I do not overwrite its declared outputs and I ask how to proceed.

**3. Questions first, without inventing content**
The standard says to ask proactively, so I send one short batch of questions right away:
- which processed dataset and variables;
- which plot type and grouping;
- the output path;
- whether any filter or subset applies;
- whether this relates to an existing figure.

I reuse whatever axes, plot type, inputs and path the user gives. I do not invent:
- variables, units, transformations or exclusions;
- statistical tests;
- a caption that states a conclusion.

While waiting I do independent work, such as validating the input and reading the schema. If the plot would need a new scientific judgment (an exclusion, outlier rule, transformation, threshold, model or test), that is not a styling choice. It needs independent critique with `scientific-critical-thinking`, or the full gate if the design changes, before any dependent work. The meeting deadline does not waive it.

**4. Implementation**
- Python is the default backend. I write an importable function under `src/<pkg>/figures/fig_<short_name>/`, with the identifier `fig_<short_name>` because the figure has no manuscript slot. A thin orchestration entry follows the repository's existing figure pattern.
- The function reads only a declared, validated file under `data/processed/` and never touches `data/raw/`.
- It runs on its own, without state left over from an earlier plotting session.
- It exports tidy figure data to `results/figure_data/fig_<name>/fig_<name>.csv`, including individual observations if the plot shows summaries. This is enough to recreate every mark.
- The PNG goes to `results/figures/fig_<name>/png/fig_<name>.png`, unless the user asked for an existing path.
- Any setting that affects the result (such as bin width or log scale) gets one owner in `config/analysis/figures.yaml`, validated by its schema and passed through `params:`. It is never a hidden default in code.
- Missing values are counted and reported, never silently dropped. The `n` behind each mark is reported.
- I avoid random jitter. If randomness is unavoidable, I use the declared `random_seed` from run.yaml and never invent one.
- Outputs are written transactionally: build in a temporary location, validate, then replace. Replacing anything other than this figure's own declared output needs explicit authorization.
- The plot carries an exploratory label. Units come from the data dictionary; if they are missing, I flag that rather than guess.
- I add focused tests sized to repository practice and run the repository's format, lint, type and test commands.

**5. Independent simplifier review**
After the code and test changes, and before delivery, I launch `research-repo-standard:research-code-simplifier` on Claude Code. The delegate reports its own provenance. I then review its diff, rerun checks, and re-render if anything changed. The time pressure is not grounds for a waiver. A waiver is possible only if launch actually fails, and only with explicit user consent recorded in the completion report.

**6. Record before presenting**
Showing the plot at the lab meeting counts as presenting results. Before that, I append an entry to docs/LAB_NOTEBOOK.md (or the existing figure record). It covers:
- the decision and its rationale and evidence;
- the authorization source (the user's request) and the affected work;
- the figure identifier, inputs, transformation, units and output path;
- its exploratory status.

ANALYSIS_PLAN.md is not amended, because no analysis decision changed.

**7. Figure QA**
- I open and visually inspect the actual PNG. File existence or a successful save is not evidence.
- I check every quantitative mark against the figure-data CSV, including counts, centers and spreads.
- I apply the relevant checklist items:
  - no overlapping or clipped labels or legends;
  - axis scales that support fair comparison;
  - correct units;
  - text readable when projected;
  - sufficient resolution;
  - no manuscript panel letter;
  - colors and encodings consistent with existing figures;
  - red/green never the only distinction;
  - `n` and center/spread documented;
  - figure data that reproduce every mark;
  - the most likely skeptical challenge disclosed.
- Items that only apply to publication are not applicable: SVG/PDF editability, 600 dpi TIFF, journal final size, assembled panel letters, and recording QA in FIGURE_CONTRACT.md.
- QA outcomes go in the figure record or notebook. If I cannot view images, that stays an explicit QA boundary, never a pass.

**8. Completion**
I check the diff and git status and preserve raw data and unrelated work. I do not commit or push unless asked. The report covers:
- what changed and how it was verified;
- the deferred publication work: promoting the figure later triggers the full publication path (pre-plot contract, `nature-figure`, exemplars, editable exports, publication QA), and calling it a draft does not waive these;
- open questions and any manual or inaccessible boundaries.
